 note = {For Part One:
This concert offers a panorama of the clavichord repertoire from a period when the instrument was still widely popular, even as the emerging piano was coming into prominence. The program opens with the famous Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach and one of his sonatas from für Kenner und Liebhaber. Daniel Gottlob Türk, the author of the renowned Klavierschule (1789), was a passionate advocate of the clavichord throughout his life. The sonatas of Nathanael Gottfried Gruner were published in 1781 and attracted an exceptionally large number of subscribers, which supported him after a tragic fire. Among the subscribers was C. P. E. Bach.

For Part Two:
Long before forging his fine reputation as a pedagogue in London, Muzio Clementi was the first keyboard player to compose, perform publicly, and sell challenging music. In keeping with the ideals of an emerging bourgeoisie, Clementi emphasized work and practice at the piano, his Toccata Op. 11 being a perfect example. Centered on a piano built by Longman, Clementi & Co., this concert explores the diversity of effects found in the leading pianist-composers of the Clementi school.},
